          "explanation": [
            {
              "text": "Chlorine is more electronegative than carbon, so the two C–C<i>l</i> bonds on carbon-2 pull electron density away from the carboxyl group (a negative inductive effect). This spreads out (delocalises) the negative charge on the carboxylate ion, making the anion more stable, and it weakens the O–H bond. Both effects make it easier to lose H<sup>+</sup>, so 2,2-dichlorobutanoic acid is the stronger acid. The two chlorines are on the carbon next to COOH, so the effect is large.",
